    "output_text": "```python\nimport heapq\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef kth_largest(nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Find the kth largest element in a list.\n    \n    Uses a min-heap of size k to efficiently find the kth largest element.\n    \n    Args:\n        nums: A list of integers.\n        k: The kth largest element to find (1-indexed).\n        \n    Returns:\n        The kth largest element in the list.\n        \n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If k is less than 1 or greater than the length of nums.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        raise ValueError(\"The list is empty.\")\n    if k < 1 or k > len(nums):\n        raise ValueError(f\"k must be between 1 and {len(nums)}, got {k}.\")\n    \n    # Use a min-heap of size k\n    # The heap will contain the k largest elements seen so far\n    # The root of the min-heap is the smallest among those k, i.e., the kth largest\n    min_heap = []\n    \n    for num in nums:\n        if len(min_heap) < k:\n            heapq.heappush(min_heap, num)\n        else:\n            # If the current number is larger than the smallest in the heap,\n            # replace the smallest with the current number\n            if num > min_heap[0]:\n                heapq.heapreplace(min_heap, num)\n    \n    # The root of the min-heap is the kth largest element\n    return min_heap[0]\n```\n\n**Explanation of Time Complexity:**\n\nThe algorithm uses a min-heap of size `k` to track the `k` largest elements seen so far.\n\n- **Heap operations:** For each of the `n` elements in the list, we perform either a `heappush` (when the heap has fewer than `k` elements) or a comparison followed by a `heapreplace` (when the heap is full and the current element is larger than the heap's minimum). Each heap operation takes O(log k) time because the heap size is bounded by `k`.\n- **Total time complexity:** O(n log k), where `n` is the length of the list and `k` is the rank of the element we're looking for.\n- **Space complexity:** O(k) for storing",
